[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-10-2-3",
    "slot_key": "Audit trail completeness and retention expectations",
    "slot_value": "Migration must define core validation and retention rules before cutover so the process is safe and auditable. Every migrated record must be validated against the source, any exceptions must be logged, and the original import evidence must be retained for audit. The minimum rule should be clear enough to test what was accepted, what was rejected, and why; if a correction happens later, the system must keep the full change history, the reason for the amendment, who approved it, and any source evidence or provider confirmation tied to that correction, preserving the prior version rather than overwriting it. Corrections to approved records should be authorized only by a limited set of privileged users, usually supervisors or designated operations leads, not general staff, and must include an attached reason plus supporting evidence such as a provider notice, audit reference, or ticket number. Unresolved items should be blocked at cutover, escalated immediately, and accompanied by a formal hold and audit note; if only one record is unresolved, the delay decision should apply to that record rather than the whole migration. Transaction history and auditability are essential, supporting control and traceability, though they are behind live operational functions. Record request intake, validation result, status changes, conflicts raised, approvals or overrides, routing activation, retry or cancellation actions, and the final outcome, including who made each decision and when; if a workaround is used, capture the reason and whether it was temporary. Staff should see the transaction identifiers, the provider names involved, the timestamps, the current status, the decision trail, and any validation or exception notes tied to the case.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-10-2-dyn-7",
    "slot_key": "Compliance access controls and traceability",
    "slot_value": "Only authorized staff should be able to view or change sensitive portability data, with traceability of access and changes; after approval and especially after routing is activated, portability records should be effectively immutable except for tightly controlled correction fields or an authorized amendment process, and only designated operations users with strong audit tracking should be able to change them. Access should be role-based so operations can see what they need, while sensitive data is masked or redacted unless a higher clearance or a specific investigation justifies full visibility.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
