    "slot_number": "slot-10-2-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Cutover and legacy record retention",
    "slot_value": "The new system becomes the system of record at cutover, while old records are retained for reference and dispute handling for as long as business, regulatory, and audit requirements call for, with the retention period defined by policy rather than left open-ended. In practice, this should be a fixed policy window; once that window ends the records can be disposed of unless there is a reason to hold them longer. Access to legacy records should be tightly restricted to authorized operations, compliance, audit, and support staff, with normal users kept in the new system only. Staff should consult the old record for historical disputes, unresolved pre-cutover transactions, regulatory inquiries, and reconciliation issues between the old and new systems; for routine post-cutover ports with a clean history in the new system, they should rely on the new record alone.",
    "operation": "refine"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-10-2-3",
    "slot_key": "Audit trail completeness and retention expectations",
    "slot_value": "Migration must define core validation and retention rules before cutover so the process is safe and auditable. Every migrated record must be validated against the source, any exceptions must be logged, and the original import evidence must be retained for audit. The minimum rule should be clear enough to test what was accepted, what was rejected, and why; if a correction happens later, the system must keep the full change history, the reason for the amendment, who approved it, and any source evidence or provider confirmation tied to that correction, preserving the prior version rather than overwriting it. Corrections to approved records should be authorized only by a limited set of privileged users, usually supervisors or designated operations leads, not general staff, and must include an attached reason plus supporting evidence such as a provider notice, audit reference, or ticket number. Unresolved items should be blocked at cutover, escalated immediately, and accompanied by a formal hold and audit note; if only one record is unresolved, the delay decision should apply to that record rather than the whole migration. Transaction history and auditability are essential, supporting control and traceability, though they are behind live operational functions. Record request intake, validation result, status changes, conflicts raised, approvals or overrides, routing activation, retry or cancellation actions, and the final outcome, including who made each decision and when; if a workaround is used, capture the reason and whether it was temporary. Staff should see the transaction identifiers, the provider names involved, the timestamps, the current status, the decision trail, and any validation or exception notes tied to the case. Any active dispute, audit, litigation, regulatory inquiry, fraud investigation, or legal hold should pause disposal until it is formally cleared, and if a case is still open or could reasonably become evidence, it should be retained beyond the normal schedule.",
