[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-10-2-3",
    "slot_key": "Audit trail completeness and retention expectations",
    "slot_value": "Migration must define core validation and retention rules before cutover so the process is safe and auditable. Every migrated record must be validated against the source, any exceptions must be logged, and the original import evidence must be retained for audit. The minimum rule should be clear enough to test what was accepted, what was rejected, and why; if a correction happens later, the system must keep the full change history, the reason for the amendment, who approved it, and any source evidence or provider confirmation tied to that correction, preserving the prior version rather than overwriting it. Corrections to approved records should be authorized only by a limited set of privileged users, usually supervisors or designated operations leads, not general staff, and must include an attached reason plus supporting evidence such as a provider notice, audit reference, or ticket number. Unresolved items should be blocked at cutover, escalated immediately, and accompanied by a formal hold and audit note; if only one record is unresolved, the delay decision should apply to that record rather than the whole migration. Transaction history and auditability are essential, supporting control and traceability, though they are behind live operational functions. Record request intake, validation result, status changes, conflicts raised, approvals or overrides, routing activation, retry or cancellation actions, and the final outcome, including who made each decision and when; if a workaround is used, capture the reason and whether it was temporary. Staff should see the transaction identifiers, the provider names involved, the timestamps, the current status, the decision trail, and any validation or exception notes tied to the case. Any active dispute, audit, litigation, regulatory inquiry, fraud investigation, or legal hold should pause disposal until it is formally cleared, and if a case is still open or could reasonably become evidence, it should be retained beyond the normal schedule. The hold should start when the matter is formally opened or when a written notice is received that identifies the case as subject to hold, whichever comes first. The opening notice or case ID should be stored against the affected records so it’s clear why disposal stopped. Exceptions to the hold audit trail should be narrow: system-generated changes may be shown without internal security details, and older actions that occurred before the hold was linked to a specific case may be visible only as a general timeline entry if they cannot reliably be tied to a person or matter. If anything is redacted, operations should still see that an action occurred, who or what role performed it if known, and the timestamp, even if the underlying sensitive note or reason text is hidden. Audit rules should document what is intentionally withheld versus what was never captured. If only part of the file needs to stay protected, the hold should apply just to the relevant records or fields, not necessarily the entire case file, as long as they can be segregated safely. The protected portion should be retained unchanged until the hold is lifted, while the non-sensitive parts should follow normal retention and disposal rules. If the file cannot be reliably split, the whole record set should remain under hold. Sensitive material should remain access-controlled and redacted in ordinary views during the extended retention period.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-5-1-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Legal retention and regulator format details",
    "slot_value": "Exact retention periods are defined by policy to satisfy business, regulatory, and audit requirements rather than left open-ended; legacy records are retained for as long as those requirements call for. In practice, the retention should be a fixed policy window. Once that window ends, the records can be disposed of unless there is a reason to hold them longer. Active disputes, audits, litigation, regulatory inquiries, fraud investigations, or legal holds should pause disposal until formally cleared. If a case is still open or could reasonably become evidence, the record should be retained beyond the normal schedule. Access to legacy records is tightly restricted to authorized operations, compliance, audit, and support staff, with normal users kept in the new system only. When a matter is formally opened or a written notice identifying the case as subject to hold is received, whichever comes first, that event should mark the start of the hold, and the opening notice or case ID should be stored against the affected records. Responsibility for placing the hold should sit with compliance, legal, audit, or fraud management depending on the matter type, and the same function should formally close the hold; operations may flag the issue but are not the final authority to start or lift it. Operations should be able to see hold status history, including the date and time it was placed or lifted, the person or role that authorized it, the stated reason or reference number tied to the case, and any related notes such as dispute ID, investigation number, or legal notice. Reporting should show the full sequence of hold actions, any changes to the affected record set, and whether the hold was active during any disposal or retention decision so staff can tell exactly who did what and whether it was properly authorized. If only part of the file needs to stay protected, the hold should apply just to the relevant records or fields when they can be safely segregated; the protected portion should remain unchanged until the hold is lifted, while the non-sensitive parts follow normal retention and disposal rules. If the file cannot be reliably split, the safer rule is to keep the whole record set under hold. Sensitive material should remain access-controlled and redacted in ordinary views during the extended retention period.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

